YGG-C21 PREREGISTRATION — REPLICATE-8 SINGLE LESION-CELL SUBSTITUTION
Parent C20 run 36255033844 valid MIXED_IDENTITY_CONTEXT with complete non-lesion preservation evidence.
Question: is the failing replicate8/original-lesion pairing locally brittle to a single lesion-cell substitution, or does failure survive small context changes before the full partner lesion is reached?
Freeze exact C20/C19 below alpha 0.134765625; levels 8..16; exact manifests, learned weights, task/scheduler, retention/maturity predicates; replicate8 identity; deterministic execution; no retraining, online adaptation, threshold redefinition, topology change, or baseline mutation.
Fixed partner: replicate1, selected before outcomes by lowest non8 identity. C20 established that every non8 partner, including replicate1, is a rescuing full-lesion exchange; no partner is outcome-selected for C21.
Design per level: reproduce original replicate8 lesion as failing anchor and full replicate1 lesion on replicate8 as rescuing anchor. Compute cells unique to replicate8 lesion and cells unique to replicate1 lesion. Enumerate every distinct one-for-one substitution formed by removing one replicate8-unique lesion cell and adding one replicate1-unique lesion cell, preserving lesion cardinality. Evaluate replicate8 only; deduplicate identical lesion sets lexicographically.
Classify EXACT_PAIRING_FRAGILE if every valid one-cell substitution rescues maturity at every level; LOCAL_CONTEXT_ROBUST if every valid one-cell substitution retains failure at every level; MIXED_LOCAL_CONTEXT if both rescue and failure occur; NO_LOCAL_DIFFERENCE if no one-cell substitution exists; ANCHOR_NOT_REPRODUCED if original failure or full-partner rescue fails; OTHER_VALID_PATTERN otherwise.
Validity: exact alpha/levels/replicate/partner; lesion cardinality preserved every arm; all preregistered unique one-cell substitutions tested; original-failure and full-partner-rescue anchors reproduced; duplicate analysis byte-identical.
Scientific negatives are valid. No post-result tuning.
